WEBVTT

0
00:00.260 --> 00:01.450
<v Krista>In this video, we're talking about</v>

1
00:01.450 --> 00:04.080
division of signed numbers, and this is going to be

2
00:04.080 --> 00:07.280
really, really similar to multiplication of signed numbers.

3
00:07.280 --> 00:08.750
We have the same chart here that we had

4
00:08.750 --> 00:10.225
in our multiplication of signed numbers video,

5
00:10.225 --> 00:12.338
except that instead of multiplication,

6
00:12.338 --> 00:13.830
we have division here.

7
00:13.830 --> 00:15.320
And what this tells us is that

8
00:15.320 --> 00:18.068
if we divide a positive number by a positive number,

9
00:18.068 --> 00:19.810
we're going to get a positive answer.

10
00:19.810 --> 00:21.928
If we divide a negative number by a negative number,

11
00:21.928 --> 00:23.870
we're going to get a positive answer.

12
00:23.870 --> 00:26.030
If, on the other hand, we divide a negative number

13
00:26.030 --> 00:28.700
by a positive number, or a positive number

14
00:28.700 --> 00:31.380
by a negative number, we're going to get a negative answer.

15
00:31.380 --> 00:33.421
In other words, if our signs are the same,

16
00:33.421 --> 00:35.190
we're going to get a positive answer.

17
00:35.190 --> 00:36.530
If our signs are different,

18
00:36.530 --> 00:38.020
we're going to get a negative answer.

19
00:38.020 --> 00:40.131
But keep in mind that this logic only works

20
00:40.131 --> 00:42.670
when we have just two numbers involved.

21
00:42.670 --> 00:45.290
So we'll talk about more than two numbers in a second.

22
00:45.290 --> 00:46.560
But let's go ahead and apply this

23
00:46.560 --> 00:48.170
to some of the examples we have here.

24
00:48.170 --> 00:49.550
So we have six divided by three.

25
00:49.550 --> 00:52.320
This is a positive six, and a positive three.

26
00:52.320 --> 00:53.540
Both of our numbers are positive.

27
00:53.540 --> 00:54.550
If we look at our chart here,

28
00:54.550 --> 00:56.585
we have a positive and a positive,

29
00:56.585 --> 00:59.230
which is going to give us a positive result,

30
00:59.230 --> 01:02.080
and that should make sense because the signs are the same.

31
01:02.080 --> 01:04.140
They're both positive, and we know from looking at this

32
01:04.140 --> 01:06.460
that if the signs are the same, we'll get a positive result.

33
01:06.460 --> 01:08.394
So positive six divided by positive three

34
01:08.394 --> 01:10.800
is going to be a positive two.

35
01:10.800 --> 01:12.370
If we go down to our last example,

36
01:12.370 --> 01:15.890
and we take a negative six divided by a negative three,

37
01:15.890 --> 01:17.440
we have a negative by a negative,

38
01:17.440 --> 01:19.100
that's going to give us a positive.

39
01:19.100 --> 01:21.130
That should make sense because the signs are the same,

40
01:21.130 --> 01:22.860
so we know our result is positive.

41
01:22.860 --> 01:25.130
So again we're going to get a positive two.

42
01:25.130 --> 01:27.000
If, on the other hand, our signs are different,

43
01:27.000 --> 01:29.359
we have a negative six and a positive three.

44
01:29.359 --> 01:31.890
Negative six divided by a positive three

45
01:31.890 --> 01:34.000
is going to give us a negative result

46
01:34.000 --> 01:37.070
because we have a negative divided by a positive.

47
01:37.070 --> 01:38.400
Those are different signs.

48
01:38.400 --> 01:39.770
The result's going to be negative.

49
01:39.770 --> 01:42.750
Here we have a positive six and a negative three.

50
01:42.750 --> 01:44.850
Different signs, one positive, one negative.

51
01:44.850 --> 01:46.886
So a positive six divided by a negative three

52
01:46.886 --> 01:49.260
is also going to give us negative two

53
01:49.260 --> 01:50.430
because the signs are different,

54
01:50.430 --> 01:51.940
we're going to get a negative answer.

55
01:51.940 --> 01:53.910
You can also think about these, instead of using

56
01:53.910 --> 01:55.970
this division sign, as fractions.

57
01:55.970 --> 01:57.584
So we know that six divided by three

58
01:57.584 --> 02:00.146
is the same as six over three.

59
02:00.146 --> 02:02.540
We know that negative six divided by three

60
02:02.540 --> 02:05.520
is the same as negative six divided by three,

61
02:05.520 --> 02:07.600
that six divided by negative three

62
02:07.600 --> 02:10.055
is the same as six over negative three,

63
02:10.055 --> 02:12.370
and that negative six divided by negative three

64
02:12.370 --> 02:15.020
is negative six over negative three.

65
02:15.020 --> 02:17.450
We can see here that we have two positives.

66
02:17.450 --> 02:19.970
Six over three, we know that's going to be equal to two.

67
02:19.970 --> 02:22.891
In this case, we have negative six over positive three.

68
02:22.891 --> 02:25.390
We have this single negative sign here.

69
02:25.390 --> 02:27.130
We can factor that negative sign

70
02:27.130 --> 02:28.470
out in front of the fraction

71
02:28.470 --> 02:31.135
and get negative six over three.

72
02:31.135 --> 02:35.229
And now this six over three looks just like this did here.

73
02:35.229 --> 02:36.910
We know this is going to be two.

74
02:36.910 --> 02:38.960
But we have to apply our negative sign here.

75
02:38.960 --> 02:40.681
So this is going to be negative two.

76
02:40.681 --> 02:42.070
We can do the same thing here.

77
02:42.070 --> 02:44.297
We can factor out the negative sign from the denominator,

78
02:44.297 --> 02:47.670
and get negative six over three,

79
02:47.670 --> 02:49.520
when we pull that negative sign out in front.

80
02:49.520 --> 02:52.040
Six over three is two, and applying the negative sign,

81
02:52.040 --> 02:53.371
we get negative two.

82
02:53.371 --> 02:56.890
Or in this case, pulling both negative signs out in front,

83
02:56.890 --> 03:00.284
what we end up with is negative one over negative one

84
03:00.284 --> 03:03.200
times six over three.

85
03:03.200 --> 03:05.350
So pulling both negatives out in front,

86
03:05.350 --> 03:06.520
we have this other fraction.

87
03:06.520 --> 03:08.370
Negative one divided by negative one

88
03:08.370 --> 03:10.810
is going to be a positive one.

89
03:10.810 --> 03:12.660
Because we have two negative signs,

90
03:12.660 --> 03:15.250
they'll cancel with each other to become a positive.

91
03:15.250 --> 03:17.360
And so we have one times six thirds,

92
03:17.360 --> 03:20.410
or just six thirds, which we know is two.

93
03:20.410 --> 03:22.900
So this same and different sign system

94
03:22.900 --> 03:25.350
works great if we have just two numbers involved.

95
03:25.350 --> 03:27.600
We're dividing one number by another number.

96
03:27.600 --> 03:29.760
But what if we have more than two numbers involved?

97
03:29.760 --> 03:31.340
What if we have, for example,

98
03:31.340 --> 03:34.289
negative two times a negative three,

99
03:34.289 --> 03:36.960
divided by a positive six?

100
03:36.960 --> 03:38.590
Here we have three numbers involved,

101
03:38.590 --> 03:41.210
negative two, negative three, and a positive six.

102
03:41.210 --> 03:43.470
In this case, we have different signs involved.

103
03:43.470 --> 03:45.630
We have a negative, a negative, and a positive.

104
03:45.630 --> 03:46.800
So those are different signs.

105
03:46.800 --> 03:48.108
And so maybe we would look at this and say,

106
03:48.108 --> 03:49.407
"Oh, they're different signs,

107
03:49.407 --> 03:50.990
"so the result's going to be negative."

108
03:50.990 --> 03:52.440
But, in fact, that's not true.

109
03:52.440 --> 03:55.741
This same and different sign logic doesn't apply

110
03:55.741 --> 03:58.059
when we have more than two numbers involved.

111
03:58.059 --> 03:59.760
When we have more than two numbers involved,

112
03:59.760 --> 04:01.754
we have to sort of upgrade our understanding,

113
04:01.754 --> 04:05.526
and say instead, that, "An even number of negative signs,

114
04:05.526 --> 04:07.744
"will always give us a positive result,

115
04:07.744 --> 04:10.227
"and an odd number of negative signs

116
04:10.227 --> 04:12.330
"will always give us a negative result."

117
04:12.330 --> 04:15.040
And this even/odd logic works for division

118
04:15.040 --> 04:16.490
of just two numbers as well.

119
04:16.490 --> 04:18.970
For example, you can see here, we had positive six

120
04:18.970 --> 04:20.410
divided by a negative three.

121
04:20.410 --> 04:21.772
We have zero negative signs.

122
04:21.772 --> 04:24.280
That's an even number of negative signs.

123
04:24.280 --> 04:26.190
So, we have an even number of negative signs,

124
04:26.190 --> 04:27.820
we know our result is going to be positive,

125
04:27.820 --> 04:29.100
and in fact, it was positive.

126
04:29.100 --> 04:30.130
It was positive two.

127
04:30.130 --> 04:31.790
Here, we have one negative sign.

128
04:31.790 --> 04:33.588
We have negative six and a positive three.

129
04:33.588 --> 04:36.634
One negative sign is an odd number of negative signs,

130
04:36.634 --> 04:38.910
so if we look down here, an odd number of negative signs,

131
04:38.910 --> 04:41.760
we should get a negative result, and in fact, we did.

132
04:41.760 --> 04:42.880
We got a negative two.

133
04:42.880 --> 04:45.471
So this even and odd number of negative signs logic

134
04:45.471 --> 04:48.070
always works, regardless of the number of values

135
04:48.070 --> 04:48.970
that you have involved.

136
04:48.970 --> 04:50.900
So we can apply that here to our

137
04:50.900 --> 04:52.580
three value division problem.

138
04:52.580 --> 04:55.420
When we take times a negative three,

139
04:55.420 --> 04:57.650
we know from multiplication of signed numbers

140
04:57.650 --> 04:59.764
that we're going to get a positive six.

141
04:59.764 --> 05:01.559
We have six in our denominator,

142
05:01.559 --> 05:04.321
and we know that six divided by six is one.

143
05:04.321 --> 05:06.354
We get a positive result.

144
05:06.354 --> 05:07.370
And that should make sense,

145
05:07.370 --> 05:09.894
because if we count the number of negative signs we have,

146
05:09.894 --> 05:12.910
we have one negative sign, two negative signs.

147
05:12.910 --> 05:14.710
That's an even number of negative signs,

148
05:14.710 --> 05:16.860
and if we look down here, we see that an even number

149
05:16.860 --> 05:19.750
of negative signs should give us a positive result.

150
05:19.750 --> 05:21.340
What if we have a different example down here?

151
05:21.340 --> 05:25.203
What if we have negative seven times a positive one

152
05:25.203 --> 05:27.933
divided by a positive seven?

153
05:27.933 --> 05:30.820
In this case, we have one negative sign,

154
05:30.820 --> 05:32.420
just the negative seven there.

155
05:32.420 --> 05:34.250
That's an odd number of negative signs,

156
05:34.250 --> 05:36.110
so our result should be negative.

157
05:36.110 --> 05:37.460
And if we do the arithmetic here,

158
05:37.460 --> 05:39.275
we see we get negative seven times one

159
05:39.275 --> 05:41.753
is going to be negative seven in our numerator.

160
05:41.753 --> 05:43.745
We have seven in our denominator.

161
05:43.745 --> 05:47.524
Negative seven divided by seven we know is a negative one,

162
05:47.524 --> 05:49.570
and we get a negative result

163
05:49.570 --> 05:51.800
because we had an odd number of negative signs.

164
05:51.800 --> 05:54.910
So when it comes to dealing with division of signed numbers

165
05:54.910 --> 05:56.988
just remember an even number of negative signs

166
05:56.988 --> 05:59.021
will give you a positive result.

167
05:59.021 --> 06:01.054
An odd number of negative signs

168
06:01.054 --> 06:03.683
will give you a negative result.